{
  "answer": "The Production Database Access SOP describes how an engineer requests, receives, and loses production database access, operating separately from the Security Policy to allow operational steps to change [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. This procedure applies to every engineer needing direct read or write access to a production database, such as on-call engineers, those debugging customer issues, or those running one-time data migrations, but excludes access through internal admin tools that enforce their own audit logs and approval flows [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. Engineers request access via the access request form on the internal tools portal, providing the specific database and table or schema, the reason, and the expected duration [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. Approvals require sign-off from the data owner and two engineering managers (with one from a different team than the requesting engineer), all recorded in the form [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. Standard access is granted for three days and expires automatically without requiring a manual revocation step, and engineers must have completed the annual security training within the last ninety days [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13, fa6a8fa3ff7c9abfd858f139e55ab66d]. During incidents, on-call engineers may use break-glass access without standard approval, which is logged and reviewed the next business day [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. All grants are logged and reviewed weekly by Security Engineering, and managers or data owners can revoke access early [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13, 91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833]. Service accounts and automated jobs follow a separate process requiring sign-off from the data owner and two engineering managers, with quarterly reviews instead of automatic three-day expiration [91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833]. This SOP governs operational details over the Security Policy where they appear to disagree [91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833].",
  "citations": [
    "da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13",
    "fa6a8fa3ff7c9abfd858f139e55ab66d",
    "91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833"
  ],
  "sufficient_evidence": true
}

{
  "answer": "The Production Database Access SOP describes the operational procedure for how an engineer requests, receives, and loses production database access [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. It applies to engineers needing direct read or write access to a production database, such as on-call engineers, those debugging customer-reported issues, or running one-time data migrations, while excluding access granted through internal admin tools with their own audit logs and approval flows [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. Standard requests are submitted through the internal tools portal and require sign-off from the data owner and two engineering managers (one from a different team) [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. Approved standard access lasts for three days and expires automatically [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. Engineers must have completed security awareness training within the last ninety days [fa6a8fa3ff7c9abfd858f139e55ab66d]. During incidents, on-call engineers can request break-glass access without standard approvals [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13]. All grants are logged and reviewed weekly by Security Engineering, and access can be revoked early by either approver alone [da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13, 91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833]. Service accounts and automated jobs follow a separate process requiring sign-off from the data owner and two engineering managers, with quarterly reviews instead of automatic three-day expiration [91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833]. This SOP is the authoritative source for operational details and governs where it differs from the Security Policy [fa6a8fa3ff7c9abfd858f139e55ab66d, 91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833].",
  "citations": [
    "da1cdb5e0e87ad69728e9d2781239d13",
    "fa6a8fa3ff7c9abfd858f139e55ab66d",
    "91d4f121536a0880dff84e83193cd833"
